===== pm --help
usage: pm [-h]
          {show,day,finding,feedback,decision,action,need,reply,doc,design,postmortem,project,sprint,task,dep,comment,record,check,sync,service,doctor,upgrade,uninstall,init,push,where,clean,export,version,prime,hook,commit} ...

pm: the write path for project records, and project actions that touch both records and the work store.

positional arguments:
  {show,day,finding,feedback,decision,action,need,reply,doc,design,postmortem,project,sprint,task,dep,comment,record,check,sync,service,doctor,upgrade,uninstall,init,push,where,clean,export,version,prime,hook,commit}
    show                compact status of open projects for agents, level by level
    day                 day pages: generated from the day's activity; nobody writes a day record
    finding             sprint findings
    feedback            feedback on pm itself
    decision            decisions: record one, or ask the owner for one
    action              actions: ask the owner to do something (run, apply), or to review a PR (--pr)
    need                needs of any kind: decisions, actions and reviews
    reply               the owner's replies from the site
    doc                 free-form dated docs
    design              design pages: create or rename one, or record why one stays as it is
    postmortem          incident postmortems
    project             projects
    sprint              sprints
    task                tasks inside open sprints
    dep                 dependencies: what an item waits on
    comment             notes on items
    record              records on the served site
    check               check that every record renders with the work store, writing nothing; the check before a commit, and the one pm commit runs
    sync                sync the work store with the remote now, as the pm service does every 10 minutes
    service             the pm service: one background process per clone serves the site, syncs the work store and pushes the records branch every 10 minutes
    doctor              compare every managed piece with what this pm writes, and the clone and worktree with what pm init makes; report each difference, exit 1 on any (in a checkout whose pin is not the version the clone's pm service runs, the work store cannot be checked: its line names the mismatch and the fix); fail when the CLI that summary_runtime in .pm/config.toml names (claude or codex) is not on PATH, since the pm service summarizes with it, and never for the other runtime, whose sessions, and so whose owner-request judge, run only where it is installed; and name a pm release newer than the pin, with its notes (or say the release list cannot be read), and the launcher's pins that no checkout on this machine pins, which pm upgrade prunes: both leave the exit code alone
    upgrade             first print the upgrade guide of each release the move crosses, after the pin up to this pm, oldest first, from the release notes this pm was built with (no network; it fails, writing nothing, when a crossed release's guide is missing); then move the pin in .pm/config.toml to this pm and rewrite every managed piece as it writes them (the fix pm doctor names for a changed one), removing the pre-package harness's and the ones an earlier pm wrote and this one retired (the records/ copy and guard workflows, the pre-commit section); writes files and prints the commit to make, never commits; then lists the clone's other worktrees whose checked-out branch pins another pm, where pm refuses every work-store command once the main checkout pins this one, each with 'merge or rebase it onto the pin move'; last, prune the launcher's pins (<data dir>/pm/pins/<X>/pm) that no checkout on this machine pins (each worktree of this clone and of every clone with a pm service unit), keeping each pin's sha256 so a later download is checked against it, and pruning none when any of those cannot be read
    uninstall           remove pm's pieces from this worktree (hook entries, .gitignore block, .pm/, an earlier pm's workflows and git hook sections in .pm/hooks) and the clone's and machine's setup (the store checkout, records/ links and an earlier pm's sparse checkouts in every worktree, the pm service, this clone's Codex writable_roots, pm's lines in .git/info/exclude, a core.hooksPath an earlier pm pointed at .pm/hooks, the work store (refused while it holds what the remote lacks; with the pm service stopped, it runs pm service run for that check)); keeps the records branch and the remote's work store (refs/pm/work); never commits
    init                install pm: the repo's files on first install (--site-url sets the public site link), then this clone, this worktree and the pm service (PORT=<n> sets its port, and on a first install the config's; PORT=<n> pm service install moves only the service's); session start runs it; never commits on the code branch
    push                sync the work store, summarize today and push the records branch; what the pm service runs every 10 minutes, not a session command
    where               list every location with its state: the store, this checkout, the work store (in a checkout whose pin is not the version the clone's pm service runs, the mismatch and the fix: merge or rebase onto main's pin move, or pm service restart), the Codex sandbox roots, the pm service, the last push, and the site; 'pm where records' prints only the store's path
    clean               list each worktree with keep or remove and the reason; --apply removes the agent worktrees (.claude/worktrees/) that no live session owns and whose work is saved
    export              print every item of the work store, one JSON object per line, ordered by id; pm init --import reads it in another clone
    version             print this pm's version, dev for an untagged build; outside any repo too
    prime               pm's rules, then pm init, pm where and pm show: the context a session starts with; the SessionStart hooks run --rules 1 to --rules 3 and --state, and an agent may run it by hand
    hook                what a runtime hook runs: the hook input JSON on stdin; installs nothing
    commit              commit your hand edits in the store, named by path, on the records branch

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit

pm runs the pm version the repo pins in .pm/config.toml: another version's release binary, downloaded once; a pin below 0.2.0 (a retired Python release) is refused. pm where names the version running and why.
===== pm show --help
usage: pm show [-h] [--json] [--project NAME] [--sprint ID] [--record PATH]
               [--section NAME] [--refresh-inbox]

Project state, level by level. Without a flag, the top level: push failures, tasks other live sessions hold, the site, the repo's pm feedback doc, and per open project a line with each open owner request and undelivered reply. Each level names the command for the next: --project, then --sprint, then --record with --section. pm show ID [--json] prints one item of any type instead: its fields, holder, blockers, children, needs and comments (pm show ID --help).

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --json           every level's data as one JSON object
  --project NAME   one open project (its name or id): its goal, owner requests in full, the repo's pm feedback doc, open sprints with their tasks, and last decisions
  --sprint ID      one sprint's frame, findings and tasks
  --record PATH    with --section: the record to read (a path, sprint id, project name or design slug)
  --section NAME   with --record: print that one section, heading included
  --refresh-inbox  first point this session's open requests at its current inbox ($CLAUDE_CODE_MESSAGING_SOCKET); the session-start hook passes it, since a resumed session binds a new one
===== pm day --help
usage: pm day [-h] {summarize} ...

positional arguments:
  {summarize}
    summarize  generate today's Today summary with claude -p or codex exec (summary_runtime); the pm service runs it

options:
  -h, --help   show this help message and exit
===== pm day summarize --help
usage: pm day summarize [-h] [--dry-run]

Summarize today's activity (what the day page shows, plus the records committed today) into records/days/<today>.summary.json, committed on the records branch; the day page shows it as Today, labelled with the time it was generated. It asks the runtime that summary_runtime in .pm/config.toml names: claude (the default) runs claude -p --model claude-haiku-5-5, codex runs codex exec --model gpt-5.6-luna; each runs from an empty temp directory with no hooks, no project instructions and no saved session, for at most 180s. Set summary_runtime = "codex" in a repo whose machines have Codex and not Claude Code. Skips when nothing happened today or the activity's digest is unchanged since the last summary. Fails, writing nothing, when the runtime's CLI is missing, fails or times out; it never falls back to the other runtime. The pm service runs it every 10 minutes.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --dry-run   print the summary it would write; write nothing
===== pm finding --help
usage: pm finding [-h] {add} ...

positional arguments:
  {add}
    add       append a bullet to a sprint's Findings; the text as one argument, or with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm finding add --help
usage: pm finding add [-h] --sprint ID [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      [TEXT ...]

Append the finding, as a bullet, to the open sprint's Findings, with its numbers. Give the text as one quoted argument, or as the body: --text="…" for one plain line, or --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF. Refuses a text that starts with --, which is an option misspelt rather than a finding.

positional arguments:
  TEXT              the finding, as one quoted argument (words given apart are joined by spaces)

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --sprint ID       the sprint's id
  --text-file PATH  the finding instead, read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the finding instead, inline, for one plain line only; backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm feedback --help
usage: pm feedback [-h] {add} ...

positional arguments:
  {add}
    add       append an entry to the repo's pm feedback doc; the text with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm feedback add --help
usage: pm feedback add [-h] [--project NAME] [--sprint ID] [--task ID]
                       [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT] [--session ID]

When pm got in the way (a confusing refusal, a missing command, a rule that cost time), say once what happened and what would have helped. Appends a dated entry with this session's id to the repo's one feedback doc, records/docs/pm-feedback.md, creating it on first use; --project, --sprint and --task tag the entry with what it is about. pm show and pm show --project link the doc.

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --project NAME    the project the feedback is about
  --sprint ID       the sprint the feedback is about
  --task ID         the task the feedback is about
  --text-file PATH  required: what happened and what would have helped; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
  --session ID      the session to record; default: this session's id from the environment
===== pm decision --help
usage: pm decision [-h] {add,need,close} ...

positional arguments:
  {add,need,close}
    add             append a decision to a project's or sprint's Decisions
    need            ask the owner for a decision under a sprint or task; its parts as flags
    close           close a decision need whose answer sets no rule, with no decision record; the answer with --text

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
===== pm decision add --help
usage: pm decision add [-h] --decision TEXT --reason TEXT
                       [--level {project,sprint}] [--project NAME]
                       [--sprint ID] [--until UNTIL] [--need ID | --confirmed]

Append a ::: decision block, dated today, to the Decisions of the named project or sprint: the --decision line, then the --reason line. Each is one line, in single quotes (in double quotes the shell runs a `code span` as a command). Choosing --level: project if a later sprint must follow it; sprint if it is about this sprint's own work; skip choices cheap to reverse. Source is agent unless --need or --confirmed. With --need, the decision is the owner's answer to that decision need: the block ends 'Answers `<need-id>`.', and the need is closed as answered with the same text unless the owner already closed it.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --decision TEXT       the decision, in one line
  --reason TEXT         why it holds, in one line
  --level {project,sprint}
                        required, no default: project if a later sprint must follow it; sprint if it is about this sprint's own work; skip choices cheap to reverse
  --project NAME        the project record name (with --level project)
  --sprint ID           the sprint's id (with --level sprint)
  --until UNTIL         a known condition to revisit the decision
  --need ID             source=owner: the decision answers this decision need, and closes it if it is open
  --confirmed           source=owner: the owner confirmed it
===== pm decision need --help
usage: pm decision need [-h] --question TEXT --fact TEXT --option LABEL TEXT
                        --cost LABEL TEXT --default LABEL REASON --title TITLE
                        --parent ID

Raise a decision need under a sprint or task. Its parts are flags, one line each, and stdin is not read: one --question, one or more --fact, two or more --option LABEL TEXT, one --cost LABEL TEXT for each option, and one --default LABEL REASON that names the option taken if the owner does not answer. Put each value in single quotes: in double quotes the shell runs a `code span` as a command. pm writes the description in one Markdown layout and refuses fewer than two options, a repeated label, an option without exactly one cost, a cost or default that names no option, and a sentence of more than 25 words. Record the answer with pm decision add --need, or close a small answer with pm decision close.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --question TEXT       what the owner decides, as one question
  --fact TEXT           a fact the owner needs to decide; repeat it for each fact
  --option LABEL TEXT   a choice: its label (letters and digits) and what it does; repeat it for each option, two or more
  --cost LABEL TEXT     what the option with this label costs; one for each option
  --default LABEL REASON
                        the option taken if the owner does not answer, and why
  --title TITLE
  --parent ID           the sprint or task the decision belongs to
===== pm decision close --help
usage: pm decision close [-h] --reason REASON [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                         need_id

Close the need with the owner's answer and the reason, as no-decision; on a need the owner already closed, only the resolution no-decision and the reason (as a comment) are set, and no answer is needed. An answer that sets a rule is recorded with pm decision add --need instead. Answers that set no rule: a name, a port, which of two equal files. If you are not sure, record a decision. A need that became moot before the owner answered has no answer to close it with: close it with pm need dismiss <id> --reason "…". If setting the resolution fails, run it again; it does not repeat the reason.

positional arguments:
  need_id           the decision need's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --reason REASON   why the answer sets no rule, in a sentence
  --text-file PATH  the owner's answer, as they gave it; required unless the owner already closed the need; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm action --help
usage: pm action [-h] {need,done} ...

positional arguments:
  {need,done}
    need       ask the owner to do something under a sprint or task, or to review a PR (--pr); description with --text
    done       close an action once you see the owner did it

options:
  -h, --help   show this help message and exit
===== pm action need --help
usage: pm action need [-h] [--title TITLE] [--parent ID] [--pr URL]
                      [--sprint ID] [--focus FOCUS] [--design SLUG]
                      [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]

Raise an action under a sprint or task. The description (--text) says what to do and why. With --pr it is a PR review instead: every sprint named must be open with its delivery report written (Outcome and 'Against "Done when"') and committed; a GitHub PR must target the main branch, as a stacked PR's merge does not reach main (raise the review on the PR that does, or retarget it); the review goes under the first and blocks its close until the PR merges and you close the review with pm action done <id> --reason "merged as <sha>"; the site's card links the PR, each sprint's record and delivery report, and the design pages named with --design plus those the sprints' records list, and shows the focus; --text then holds optional extra context. When a design page's covers: match a file the PR changes (GitHub's pull request files API, through gh; a renamed file's old path counts too), it refuses until one of the sprints updated the page and links it in its Design pages, or waived it with pm design unchanged; the review's text then links each covering page. Close it with pm action done once you see it done.

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE     required without --pr; with --pr, default: Review PR #<n>
  --parent ID       the sprint or task the action belongs to (required without --pr; not allowed with it)
  --pr URL          the pull request to review; requires --sprint and --focus
  --sprint ID       with --pr: an open sprint the PR delivers, its report written (repeatable)
  --focus FOCUS     with --pr: what to look at first: risky changes, open choices
  --design SLUG     with --pr: a design page behind the PR (repeatable, optional)
  --text-file PATH  without --pr, required: what the owner should do and why; with --pr, optional extra context; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm action done --help
usage: pm action done [-h] --reason REASON need_id

Close an action once you see the owner did it. A PR review closes with --reason "merged as <sha>" once the PR is on main. A review closed without it, as when its PR merged into a stacked base, takes it later the same way: <sha> must be on the remote's main branch (pm fetches it first), and the old reason stays after the new one. Any other closed action is refused.

positional arguments:
  need_id          the action's id

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --reason REASON  what showed you it is done (a merged PR, a command's output)
===== pm need --help
usage: pm need [-h] {edit,dismiss} ...

positional arguments:
  {edit,dismiss}
    edit          rewrite an open action's or decision need's body before the owner replies
    dismiss       close an open need as dismissed: a [TEST] need, a replaced review, or one that became moot

options:
  -h, --help      show this help message and exit
===== pm need edit --help
usage: pm need edit [-h] [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT] [--question TEXT]
                    [--fact TEXT] [--option LABEL TEXT] [--cost LABEL TEXT]
                    [--default LABEL REASON]
                    need_id

Rewrite the body of an open need that holds no reply yet: an action's description (--text), or a decision need's parts, given as pm decision need takes them (--question, --fact, --option, --cost, --default; pm decision need --help), which pm lays out and checks the same way. The title stays. Refuses a closed need, a need that holds a reply (the owner answered the body it had: raise a new need and dismiss this one with pm need dismiss), and a PR review, whose card pm builds from its PR, sprints and focus.

positional arguments:
  need_id               the open need's id

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --text-file PATH      an action's new description: what the owner should do and why; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT           the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
  --question TEXT       a decision need's question
  --fact TEXT           a decision need's fact; repeat it for each fact
  --option LABEL TEXT   a decision need's choice; two or more
  --cost LABEL TEXT     what the option with this label costs; one for each option
  --default LABEL REASON
                        the option taken if the owner does not answer, and why
===== pm need dismiss --help
usage: pm need dismiss ID --reason REASON

Close an open need as dismissed, with no answer: a [TEST] need, a replaced review, or a decision or action that became moot. pm sprint close skips a dismissed review.

options:
      --reason string   why it is dismissed (required)
===== pm reply --help
usage: pm reply [-h] {read,add} ...

positional arguments:
  {read,add}
    read      print the owner's replies and reviews' merges that did not reach your session
    add       the owner's answer to an open need, at a shell, as a reply on the site writes; the answer with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm reply read --help
usage: pm reply read [-h] [ID ...]

Print the requests' site replies not yet delivered and their reviews' PR merges not yet reported, with what to do next, and mark them delivered; it does not wait. The pm service pushes each reply and merge into the inbox of the session that raised the request when it can; what it could not deliver (the session had ended, or has no inbox) waits here and is flagged by pm show. Without ids, this session's open requests ($CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID or $CODEX_SESSION_ID, as pm task claim takes it); with ids, those, closed ones too.

positional arguments:
  ID          a decision need, action or review (default: this session's)

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm reply add --help
usage: pm reply add ID (--text TEXT | --text-file FILE)

The owner's answer to an open need, at a shell: a reply, as a reply on the site writes. The need stays open; the session that raised it reads the reply and records it, which closes the need.

options:
      --text string        the answer, as one argument
      --text-file string   the answer, from a file; - reads stdin, from a pipe or heredoc only
===== pm doc --help
usage: pm doc [-h] {new,rename} ...

positional arguments:
  {new,rename}
    new         create records/docs/<today>-<slug>.md; body with --text
    rename      rename a doc, its slug or title or both, and rewrite every record's links to it

options:
  -h, --help    show this help message and exit
===== pm doc new --help
usage: pm doc new [-h] --title TITLE (--bead ID | --project NAME)
                  [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                  slug

positional arguments:
  slug              what the doc is for, lowercase words joined by '-'

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --bead ID         the sprint or task the doc belongs to
  --project NAME    the project the doc belongs to
  --text-file PATH  required: the doc's Markdown body; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm doc rename --help
usage: pm doc rename [-h] [--to NEW_SLUG] [--title TITLE] doc

Rename a doc in one records commit: --to moves records/docs/<date>-<slug>.md to records/docs/<date>-<new>.md (the date prefix stays), --title sets its title: header; give one or both. Every record's Markdown link to the doc (../docs/<name>.md, or <name>.md beside it; .html too; anchors and link titles kept; none in code) is rewritten to the new name, and a link whose text is the old title takes the new one. Old URLs get no redirect. The repo's feedback doc is not renamed.

positional arguments:
  doc            the doc: records/docs/<date>-<slug>.md, <date>-<slug>, or <slug> when one doc has it

options:
  -h, --help     show this help message and exit
  --to NEW_SLUG  the new slug, lowercase words joined by '-', without the date
  --title TITLE  the new title
===== pm design --help
usage: pm design [-h] {new,rename,unchanged} ...

positional arguments:
  {new,rename,unchanged}
    new                 create records/design/<slug>.md with every template section
    rename              rename a design page, its slug or title or both, and rewrite every record's links and waivers to it
    unchanged           record why a design page stays as it is although the sprint's PR changes code it covers

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm design new --help
usage: pm design new [-h] --title TITLE --project NAME slug

Create a design page with every section of the template, each with its prompt line and "None yet."; then edit it by hand. A page covers one area and holds its final state; decisions and plans go in the project or sprint record. Put no date in the slug. When a page grows to cover several areas, split it into sub pages and keep a short summary per area linking them. Name the code the page is about in its header, covers: [internal/records/**, prime.md]: path patterns relative to the repo root (doublestar globs: * within one path segment, ** as a whole segment for any depth). pm check fails on a pattern that matches no tracked file; pm action need --pr and pm sprint close refuse a PR that changes a covered file until the sprint updates the page or runs pm design unchanged.

positional arguments:
  slug            what the design is, lowercase words joined by '-'

options:
  -h, --help      show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --project NAME  the project the design belongs to
===== pm design rename --help
usage: pm design rename [-h] [--to NEW_SLUG] [--title TITLE] slug

Rename a design page in one records commit: --to moves records/design/<slug>.md to records/design/<new>.md, --title sets its title: header; give one or both. Every record's Markdown link to the page (../design/<slug>.md, or <slug>.md from another design page; .html too; anchors and link titles kept; none in code) is rewritten to the new slug, a link whose text is the old title takes the new one, and each pm design unchanged waiver of the page names the new slug. Old URLs get no redirect. Refused while an open PR review need names the page (pm action need --design): close or dismiss it first.

positional arguments:
  slug           the design page's slug (records/design/<slug>.md)

options:
  -h, --help     show this help message and exit
  --to NEW_SLUG  the new slug, lowercase words joined by '-'
  --title TITLE  the new title
===== pm design unchanged --help
usage: pm design unchanged [-h] --sprint ID --reason REASON slug

Record, as a sprint decision of source agent, that the design page stays as it is for the sprint's code changes, and why. pm action need --pr and pm sprint close refuse a PR that changes a file a design page covers (its covers: header) until one of the PR's sprints either updated the page (a pm commit of it since the sprint opened, and a link to it in the sprint record's Design pages section) or recorded this. Use it when the change leaves what the page says true; when it does not, edit the page to the new state instead.

positional arguments:
  slug             the design page's slug (records/design/<slug>.md)

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --sprint ID      the open sprint whose PR changes the covered code
  --reason REASON  why the page stays true, in one line
===== pm postmortem --help
usage: pm postmortem [-h] {new} ...

positional arguments:
  {new}
    new       create records/postmortems/<today>-<slug>.md with every template section

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm postmortem new --help
usage: pm postmortem new [-h] --title TITLE (--sprint ID | --project NAME)
                         slug

Create a postmortem with every section of the template, each with its prompt line and "None yet."; then write it by hand. Due for an incident that cost more than a day, or broke other sessions or the owner's view. Write it once the incident is fixed.

positional arguments:
  slug            what broke, lowercase words joined by '-'

options:
  -h, --help      show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --sprint ID     the sprint the incident hit, open or closed
  --project NAME  the project the incident hit
===== pm project --help
usage: pm project [-h] {open,close} ...

positional arguments:
  {open,close}
    open        create a project epic and record; Goal with --text
    close       close a project epic at the committed records

options:
  -h, --help    show this help message and exit
===== pm project open --help
usage: pm project open [-h] --title TITLE [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                       name

Create a project epic and its record, with the Goal from --text. The owner confirms the goal in their own words before you open the project; a request from the owner that states the goal confirms it.

positional arguments:
  name

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --text-file PATH  required: the project's Goal; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm project close --help
usage: pm project close [-h] name

Close a project epic at the committed records. Close every sprint first. Write the record's '## Outcome' first, by hand: the results against the goal in numbers, what was learned, what was retired, and links to the sprints' delivery reports.

positional arguments:
  name

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm sprint --help
usage: pm sprint [-h] {open,close,edit,move} ...

positional arguments:
  {open,close,edit,move}
    open                create a sprint epic and record; frame with --text ('## Goal', '## Scope' with **In:**/**Out:**, '## Done when')
    close               close a sprint epic once its report is written, every task is closed and each PR review is closed as merged
    edit                rename an open sprint; reason with --text
    move                move an open sprint, with its tasks, frame, decisions and findings, to another open project; reason with --text

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm sprint open --help
usage: pm sprint open [-h] --title TITLE [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      project

positional arguments:
  project           project record name, e.g. pm-harness

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --text-file PATH  required: the frame, '## Goal', '## Scope' and '## Done when' sections; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm sprint close --help
usage: pm sprint close [-h] [--merged SHA] [--pr URL] sprint_id

Close a sprint epic at the committed records. Refuses until the Delivery report is written and every task is closed, and each PR review naming the sprint is closed with pm action done <id> --reason "merged as <sha>" once its PR is on main; it asks GitHub only for the files each PR changes, and refuses, as pm action need --pr does, while a design page covering one is neither updated nor waived with pm design unchanged (with --merged and no --pr, the files a merge commit brought against its first parent; a commit with one parent, a squash or rebase merge, is refused there, as its delivery cannot be told, so give --pr). With reviews, it stamps 'Merged as <sha> (PR #N).' into the Outcome after the verdict and commits it on the records branch; the close reason names the records commit. A PR an agent merged has no review: --merged SHA [--pr URL] gives its merge commit, which must be on the remote's main branch (pm fetches it first), and stamps the same line ('Merged as <sha>.' without --pr); it refuses a sprint that holds a PR review, whose close stamps the merge instead. Both forms then fetch the remote's main and refuse while a design page the sprint's Design pages list, or the gate names for its PRs, has a covers: pattern the sprint added or changed that matches no file there, naming page and pattern: fix the pattern, or merge the PR that adds the file first (pm check lists such a pattern as waiting on unmerged work). A pattern is the sprint's unless the page held it at the last records commit before the sprint opened; so another sprint's pending pattern from before then does not hold it, and a page made during the sprint is all its own. Only the Outcome's first paragraph becomes the close reason.

positional arguments:
  sprint_id     the sprint's id

options:
  -h, --help    show this help message and exit
  --merged SHA  the merge commit of the sprint's PR, merged with no PR review: a commit on the remote's main branch
  --pr URL      with --merged: the merged pull request, named in the stamp
===== pm sprint edit --help
usage: pm sprint edit [-h] --title TITLE [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      sprint_id

Give an open sprint a new title: its work-store title, which keeps its 'Sprint <n>: ' prefix, and its record's title header, in one records commit that also adds the rename as a source=agent decision to the sprint's Decisions. The work store is written first; when the records step fails, the old title is put back. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines. Refuses a closed sprint, an unchanged title, and a title that carries its own 'Sprint <n>: ' prefix. A scope change is still a sprint decision.

positional arguments:
  sprint_id         the sprint's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE     the new title, without 'Sprint <n>: '
  --text-file PATH  required: why the title changes, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm sprint move --help
usage: pm sprint move [-h] --to PROJECT [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      sprint_id

Move an open sprint to another open project. Its id stays, so its tasks, needs and holders stay as they are and the old id shows the sprint in its new place; it takes the project's next sprint number, and its record moves to records/sprints/<project>-<number>.md. The work store is written first: the new parent and number, and a move note on the sprint that keeps its old project and number, so the old project never reuses that number and the old record path still finds the record. Then one records commit renames the record and adds the move as a source=agent decision to both projects' records. If the records step fails or is cut short, run the same command again: it finds the move in the work store and writes the records step alone, with the reason and the date (UTC) the move note holds, so a rerun on a clone whose records have not yet synced another clone's finished step writes that same step, which the records sync then drops. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines. Refuses a closed sprint, a closed project, the project the sprint is in, and a move to another project while the last move's records step has not run.

positional arguments:
  sprint_id         the sprint's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --to PROJECT      the open project the sprint moves to, by record name, e.g. pm-harness
  --text-file PATH  required: why the sprint moves, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm task --help
usage: pm task [-h] {add,close,claim,move,ready,edit,release} ...

positional arguments:
  {add,close,claim,move,ready,edit,release}
    add                 create a task in an open sprint, or a sub-task (--parent); description with --text (optional)
    close               close a task with a reason naming its commit, or drop it
    claim               claim a task for this agent session
    move                move a task to another open sprint, or into a sprint from directly under a project; reason with --text
    ready               list the tasks an agent can claim now
    edit                set a task's title or description; description with --text
    release             clear the holder of a task this session holds

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm task add --help
usage: pm task add [-h] --sprint ID --title TITLE [--text-file PATH |
                   --text TEXT]

Create a task in an open sprint. pm task add --parent TASK --title TITLE, in place of --sprint, adds a sub-task under the open task TASK instead (pm task add --parent TASK --help).

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --sprint ID       the open sprint's id
  --title TITLE
  --text-file PATH  optional: the task's description; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm task close --help
usage: pm task close [-h] [--reason REASON] [--commit REF] [--dropped] task_id

Close a task as done. The reason ends with what holds the work: '(commit <hash>)' for HEAD if it was committed after the task started, else the --commit given: a commit of this repo, OWNER/REPO@SHA for another repo's commit, or a PR URL, the last two resolved with gh; one that does not resolve, or a PR closed without merging, is refused. Without --commit, a warning when HEAD is older than the task or the working tree has uncommitted changes. --dropped closes it as not done instead (resolution dismissed, shown as dropped and left out of a sprint's task counts), with a required --reason and no commit; a task it blocks no longer waits on it. Refuses a task that another live session holds, as pm task claim does; that session closes it or releases it with pm task release.

positional arguments:
  task_id          the task's id

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --reason REASON  what was done (default: Done); with --dropped, required: why it is not done
  --commit REF     what holds the work: a commit of this repo, OWNER/REPO@SHA or a PR URL (default: HEAD, if newer than the task)
  --dropped        close it as not done: no commit, a reason required
===== pm task claim --help
usage: pm task claim [-h] [--session ID] task_id

Claim an open task: make the session (--session, else $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID or Codex's $CODEX_SESSION_ID, else its $CODEX_THREAD_ID's root thread; with both runtimes' set, that of the nearest Claude Code or Codex process pm runs under) its holder, with the time. Refuses when another live session holds it: one whose transcript was written in the last 30 minutes. A subagent shares its session's id, so it may claim what its session holds. Refuses in the main checkout, since agents change code only in a worktree of their own, and says how to make one.

positional arguments:
  task_id       the task's id

options:
  -h, --help    show this help message and exit
  --session ID  the session to record; default: this session's id from the environment
===== pm task move --help
usage: pm task move [-h] --to SPRINT_ID [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                    task_id

Move an open task to another open sprint and record the scope change as a source=agent decision in the sprint it leaves. A task filed directly under a project (the site's Not in a sprint) moves into one of that project's open sprints, and the decision records the scope added in the sprint it joins. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines.

positional arguments:
  task_id           the task's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --to SPRINT_ID    the open sprint the task moves to
  --text-file PATH  required: why the task moves, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm task ready --help
usage: pm task ready [--sprint ID] [--json]

The tasks an agent can claim now: open, under open ancestors, held by no live session, with no open blocker of their own or an ancestor's. By sprint number, then id; tasks directly under a project last. A task whose holder is not live is ready and marked a stale holder; pm task claim takes it over.

options:
      --json            the ready tasks as a JSON array of items
      --sprint string   only the tasks under this sprint
===== pm task edit --help
usage: pm task edit ID [--title TITLE] [--text TEXT | --text-file FILE]

Set a task's title, its description (the body: --text, or --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF), or both. A scope change is still pm task move or a sprint decision.

options:
      --text string        the new description, as one argument
      --text-file string   the new description, from a file; - reads stdin, from a pipe or heredoc only
      --title string       the new title
===== pm task release --help
usage: pm task release ID

Clear the holder of a task this session ($CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, $CODEX_SESSION_ID or $CODEX_THREAD_ID, as pm task claim takes it) holds.

options:
===== pm dep --help
usage: pm dep [-h] {add,rm} ...

positional arguments:
  {add,rm}
    add       make an item, and every task under it, wait until a blocker closes
    rm        remove a blocker

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm dep add --help
usage: pm dep add ID --on BLOCKER

BLOCKER blocks ID: ID, and every task under it, waits until BLOCKER closes. Refuses a blocker that does not exist and a cycle over blocked_by, ancestors included.

options:
      --on string   the blocker (required)
===== pm dep rm --help
usage: pm dep rm ID --on BLOCKER

BLOCKER no longer blocks ID.

options:
      --on string   the blocker (required)
===== pm comment --help
usage: pm comment [-h] {add} ...

positional arguments:
  {add}
    add       add a note to an item; the note with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm comment add --help
usage: pm comment add ID (--text TEXT | --text-file FILE)

Add a note to an item: --text, or --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF. Its author is this session, or owner when no session runs the command.

options:
      --text string        the note, as one argument
      --text-file string   the note, from a file; - reads stdin, from a pipe or heredoc only
===== pm record --help
usage: pm record [-h] {link} ...

positional arguments:
  {link}
    link      print a record's page URL on the served site; the only link to give for a record

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm record link --help
usage: pm record link [-h] [--local] target

Print the URL of a record's page on the site the pm service serves on localhost:$PORT (default: the installed service's port, else the port in .pm/config.toml), printed with the repo's site_url instead when .pm/config.toml sets one; --local prints the localhost URL even then, for a check on this machine that the public site's login would stop (a headless browser). The pm service's pages follow the records within 10 s and state their data's age, so no render step is needed. Fails with the command that fixes it when nothing serves there, or when what answers is not the pm service for this store.

positional arguments:
  target      a sprint or project id, a project name, a design slug, or a record path (records/<…>.md; records/ and .md optional)

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --local     print http://127.0.0.1:<port>/…, the URL the service answers on here, even when site_url is set
===== pm check --help
usage: pm check [-h]

Check that every record renders with the work store, writing nothing; pm commit runs the same check. It lists the tracked source files no design page covers: those the path patterns of design_sources in .pm/config.toml match (e.g. design_sources = ["internal/**", "prime.md"]; unset, no list), and fails on a design_sources pattern that matches no tracked file. It also lists each design page's covers: pattern that matches no file git tracks in the main checkout or this worktree, as waiting on unmerged work, and passes: a sprint brings its page to the new state before its PR merges, so a page names a file before main has it; pm sprint close holds a sprint to the remote's main only for the patterns it added or changed while open. A malformed pattern fails.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm sync --help
usage: pm sync

Sync the work store with the repo's remote now: the pm service pulls, resolves conflicts by the merge rules and pushes, as it does every 10 minutes. A conflict no rule settles fails, names the item and field, and leaves the store as it was.

options:
===== pm service --help
usage: pm service [-h] {install,status,restart,stop,logs,run} ...

The pm service: one supervised background process per clone, `pm service run` in the main checkout. It
holds the work store, which every pm command reaches through its socket, .pm/run/work.sock, and serves the
site live from the records store and the work store (a page is at most 10 s behind them), delivers
the owner's site replies and reviewed PRs' merges (GitHub polled every 60 s) into the sessions that
raised them, and syncs the work store, pushes today's summary and the records branch every
600 s, the first push 600 s after it starts. Sessions push neither.

Supervisor: it starts the service at login and again after a crash. A launchd agent with KeepAlive on
macOS (~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.pm.<dir>.<hash>.plist); a systemd user service with Restart=always
on Linux ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user/local.pm.<dir>.<hash>.service, ~/.config by default). On a
machine with neither there is no service, and install refuses.

Unit: runs the installed pm (`<bin dir>/pm service run`, the bin dir $PM_BIN_DIR, else ~/.local/bin; pm init
copies pm there) in the main checkout, with the PATH install ran with (git must be on it, and uv
for a Python pin) and PORT. The installed pm runs the version the main checkout pins: another Go version's release binary, a Python version
through uv.

Port: $PORT, else the installed unit's port, else `port` in .pm/config.toml. Installing again keeps the unit's
port. A second clone of the repo on this machine needs its own: PORT=<n> pm service install.

State and logs, in <main checkout>/.pm/run/ (never committed): service.log (pm service logs), push.json
(each push step's last outcome), push.log (a line per step per run), push.lock (one push at a time).

Health (pm service status, pm where): the supervisor holds the unit, and the site answers on its port
with X-PM-Store naming this clone's store and X-PM-Version naming this pm's build. Status also
flags a push step that failed or has not succeeded for 1800 s.

Stale build: every 1 s the service rereads the pin in .pm/config.toml; once it pins another version (a
pull after pm upgrade) the service exits and the supervisor starts the installed pm again, which runs the new
pin. A service that answers on another build than the running pm, the pinned one, is stale in pm where, pm
service status and pm doctor. Fix: pm init (it copies this pm into the bin dir when another is there, then
installs the service); when the installed pm already is this build, pm service install or pm service restart.
A unit that runs another program than the installed pm (Python pm's unit runs the pm uv tool's interpreter)
is stale there too, and session start leaves it: pm service install rewrites it.

Agents: pm prime carries pm where's service line and push state, and pm show warns when a push needs
attention. When the service is down, run pm service restart; if that fails, raise an action for the
owner (pm action need) and add a bug task (pm task add). pm init installs the service; pm service stop
stops it and keeps it stopped, at login and at session start, until pm service restart or pm init;
pm uninstall stops it and removes its unit.

positional arguments:
  {install,status,restart,stop,logs,run}
    install             install and start this clone's service (launchd on macOS, systemd on Linux), or update it; a no-op once installed and current
    status              whether the service is up and its site answers for this store, and its pushes; non-zero when either needs attention
    restart             restart the service and wait for its site to answer; when it fails, raise an action for the owner and add a bug task
    stop                stop this clone's service and keep it stopped, at login and at session start, until pm service restart or pm init
    logs                print the end of the service's log, <main checkout>/.pm/run/service.log
    run                 the service's process, run by its supervisor: serve the site on localhost:$PORT and push every 10 minutes

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm service install --help
usage: pm service install [-h]

Install the pm service under the machine's supervisor, which starts it at login and restarts it after a crash: a launchd agent with KeepAlive on macOS, a systemd user service on Linux; refused on a machine with neither. The unit runs the installed pm (refused unless it is this pm's build: run pm init) with the current PATH (git must be on it) and serves on $PORT, else the port it was installed with, else the port in .pm/config.toml; give a second clone of the repo its own port once with PORT=<n> pm service install. Installing again rewrites a changed unit and restarts a service on another build. It waits 15 s for the site to answer for this store and fails when it does not (another clone's service on the port, say). pm init runs it.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service status --help
usage: pm service status [-h]

Print the service's health line (as pm where does), each push step's last outcome, every push problem and the log's path. Up means the supervisor holds the unit and the site answers on its port with X-PM-Store naming this store and X-PM-Version naming this pm's build; a push problem is a step whose last run failed or that has not succeeded for 1800 s. Exits non-zero when the service is down, stale or a push needs attention; each line names the command that fixes it.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service restart --help
usage: pm service restart [-h]

Restart the installed service, or load it when the supervisor does not hold it (enabling it first when pm service stop stopped it), then wait 15 s for the site to answer for this store. The fix when pm where shows the service down. Refused when it is not installed (run pm service install); when it fails, read pm service logs, raise an action for the owner (pm action need) and add a bug task (pm task add).

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service stop --help
usage: pm service stop [-h]

Disable the service at its supervisor and stop it (systemd: systemctl --user disable --now; launchd: launchctl disable, then bootout), then wait 15 s until neither the work store's socket nor the site answers, and say so; fails naming what still answers (a pm service run started by hand). The supervisor's disabled state is the record of the stop: the supervisor starts it neither at login nor after a crash, session start (pm init --session-start) leaves it stopped and its state names pm service restart, and pm where, pm service status and pm doctor show it stopped. Meanwhile every pm command that reads or writes work items refuses; pm uninstall still checks for unsynced work, through a pm service run of its own. A typed pm init or pm service restart enables and starts it again. Refused when it is not installed.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service logs --help
usage: pm service logs [-h] [-n LINES]

Print the end of <main checkout>/.pm/run/service.log, where the service writes stdout and stderr: a line per request, refresh and reply write with its timings, and any error that stopped it. The push's own log is .pm/run/push.log beside it.

options:
  -h, --help         show this help message and exit
  -n, --lines LINES  how many lines (default 50)
===== pm service run --help
usage: pm service run [-h]

Serve the site on localhost:$PORT (default: port in .pm/config.toml): a page is at most 10 s behind the records and the work store and states its data's age; an open page never reloads itself but shows within ~10 s that newer data exists, loaded on reload. Every 10 minutes, the first 10 after start, run pm push. Exits once .pm/config.toml pins another pm version, so the supervisor starts the installed pm again, which runs the new pin. The supervisor runs it; run it by hand only to debug, or on another PORT.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm doctor --help
usage: pm doctor [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm upgrade --help
usage: pm upgrade [-h] [--to X]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --to X      the version to move to: the running pm's (the default), or another, which the installed pm launches to make the move; without it pm upgrade refuses a pin newer than the running pm (--to the pin rewrites the pieces at it)
===== pm uninstall --help
usage: pm uninstall [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm init --help
usage: pm init [-h] [--session-start] [--site-url URL] [--import-bd FILE |
               --import FILE]

Install pm, doing only what is missing. Repo, on first install only (no .pm/config.toml yet): write .pm/ (config.toml, README.md, .gitignore), pm's hook entries in .claude/settings.json and .codex/hooks.json, the Codex rule .codex/rules/pm.rules (pm runs outside Codex's sandbox) and pm's .gitignore lines; create the records branch with an empty store and push it when the remote has none; print the commit to make. After that pm init leaves the repo's files alone: pm doctor reports a changed or missing piece and pm upgrade rewrites it. Clone and worktree, every run: copy this pm into the bin dir when another is there, take out Beads' hook entries and CLAUDE.md block, unset a core.hooksPath that names .pm/hooks, where an earlier pm kept its git hooks, while nothing but pm's sections is there (pm installs none; a hooks path elsewhere, or one holding hooks of yours, stays), list .pm/store/ and .pm/run/ in .git/info/exclude, check out the records store at <main checkout>/.pm/store/records if missing, link this worktree's records/ to it and turn off the sparse checkout an earlier pm set once HEAD and the index track no records/, add the clone's .git and the stores to the writable roots of $CODEX_HOME/config.toml and the store to this worktree's .claude/settings.local.json, then install the pm service (pm service install) and have it attach the work store, which only the service opens (cloned from the remote's refs/pm/work, or created and pushed). Session start runs it in every worktree, and starts an installed service that does not answer; once all is set up it prints 'already set up'. Site port: a new repo's config gets $PORT, else the first free port from 8000 up that no pm service unit on this machine names; a clone's service serves on $PORT, else its unit's port, else the config's. Refuses, writing nothing, when another server holds that port, and names a free one: PORT=<n> pm init. .pm/config.toml, tracked: version (the pm every session must run; pm upgrade moves it), remote and main_branch (origin and its default branch), port (the site port) and site_url (--site-url); with site_url set, access_team (the Cloudflare Access team domain, <team>.cloudflareaccess.com) and access_aud (the Access application's AUD tag), set by hand: the site serves a request to site_url's host, or any request Cloudflare's edge forwarded (Cf-Connecting-IP), only when its Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion verifies against them (RS256 by the team's keys, aud, iss, unexpired), and refuses every such request while either is missing; pm doctor names a missing one, and the service reads them when it starts.

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --session-start   what session start runs, without $PORT: install the pm service when it is missing and start an installed one that does not answer, so a session in a clone brings its service up; one pm service stop stopped is left stopped and reported (pm service restart starts it); a running one, a stale one included, is left as it is and reported (pm service restart restarts it)
  --site-url URL    the site's public base URL (a tunnel to the pm service), written to site_url in .pm/config.toml for you to commit: every link pm prints (pm record link, pm show, pm where) uses it instead of http://localhost:<port>, and the site serves its host besides localhost, behind Cloudflare Access (access_team, access_aud); '' clears it
  --import-bd FILE  only import the bd export in FILE into this clone's work store, through the pm service, as one commit; refuses a store that holds any item, and an export it cannot map; nothing else runs
  --import FILE     only import the pm export in FILE (what pm export printed in another clone) into this clone's work store, ids kept, as --import-bd does
===== pm push --help
usage: pm push [-h]

Sync the work store with the remote, then summarize today (pm day summarize only when today's activity changed), then push the records branch when the store is ahead of <remote>/records: fetch, rebase onto it if it moved (under the store lock; a rebase that stops is aborted, leaving the store as it was), push. Each step has a 120s timeout; a second run while one holds the lock exits at once. Each step's outcome goes to <clone>/.pm/run/push.json (read by pm show, pm where, pm service status and the site) and <clone>/.pm/run/push.log, a line per step.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm where --help
usage: pm where [-h] [{records}]

positional arguments:
  {records}   print only this location's path

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm clean --help
usage: pm clean [-h] [--apply]

List every worktree of this clone with keep or remove and the reason; a dry run unless --apply. Only an agent worktree, under <main checkout>/.claude/worktrees/, is ever removed; never the main checkout, the records store (.pm/store/records) or the worktree pm clean runs in. Owned, so kept: a worktree Claude Code locked for a live process (`claude <kind> <name> (pid N start T)` in .git/worktrees/<name>/locked; the lock is stale when pid N is gone or, on Linux, started at another time than T; on macOS a live pid owns it), a worktree someone else locked, and one a live session used: an entry of a Claude Code transcript (<config dir>/projects/) or a Codex rollout ($CODEX_HOME/sessions/) written in the last 30 minutes whose directory is in the worktree or whose tool call names the worktree's path (a subagent runs in its parent's directory). Removed, when clean (no change, no untracked file; its ignored files go with it, as git worktree remove deletes them) and holding no other worktree: one whose commits are on <remote>/<main branch> (its branch deleted too), one whose branch a PR merged into <main branch> took at its tip (a squash merge, asked of gh; kept when gh is missing or fails; its branch deleted too), and one whose branch has nothing beyond where it was pushed: its upstream, unless that is the main branch, else <remote>/<branch> (its branch kept, as it is not merged). Everything else is kept: uncommitted changes, commits never pushed. The branches compare with <remote>/<main branch> as last fetched: run git fetch first. --apply takes a stale lock off, runs git worktree remove (never --force, so git refuses a worktree that changed meanwhile; for a worktree whose directory is gone it drops git's entry), and deletes a merged branch at the tip it judged. It refuses, removing nothing, when a recent transcript cannot be read. Exits 1 when a removal failed, naming why on its line.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --apply     remove the worktrees the dry run lists as remove; without it pm clean changes nothing
===== pm export --help
usage: pm export [-h] [--store DIR]

Print every item of this clone's work store, one JSON object per line, ordered by id. pm init --import FILE in another clone reads it, to move a project's items into a new repo's store.

options:
  -h, --help   show this help message and exit
  --store DIR  the work store at DIR (<main checkout>/.pm/store/work), read through the pm service of the clone DIR belongs to, without the repo's config
===== pm version --help
usage: pm version [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm prime --help
usage: pm prime [-h] [--rules N | --state | --subagent] [--hook-json]

options:
  -h, --help   show this help message and exit
  --rules N    only chunk N of the rules and the command list, under a title naming its sections: one hook each on SessionStart and SubagentStart, since Claude Code passes a hook's text inline only up to 10,000 characters
  --state      only pm init, pm where and pm show, cut at a line to 10,000 characters: the last SessionStart hook; in a Codex session it opens with a line naming the pm hooks Codex skips, untrusted or changed since trusted (codex app-server, asked again only when .codex/hooks.json or your Codex trust changed)
  --subagent   only pm's git rule for agents, one line: the last SubagentStart hook, beside the rules chunks
  --hook-json  read the SessionStart or SubagentStart input on stdin and print the hook's JSON envelope, as Claude Code and Codex read it
===== pm hook --help
usage: pm hook [-h]
               {stop,owner-request,ask,git-post-checkout,git-pre-commit} ...

positional arguments:
  {stop,owner-request,ask,git-post-checkout,git-pre-commit}
    stop                Stop: block once while records this session's tool calls name are uncommitted in the store, leaving out a record that only a still-running subagent's prompt names
    owner-request       Stop: block once while the reply asks the owner for something no open need or action of this session covers
    ask                 PermissionRequest and PostToolUse of AskUserQuestion (Claude Code), PostToolUse of request_user_input_async and UserPromptSubmit (Codex): raise the session's ask-tool question as a question need the owner can answer on the site too, and close it with the first answer
    git-post-checkout   retired: does nothing; an earlier pm's section in .pm/hooks/post-checkout runs it until pm init unsets core.hooksPath or pm upgrade removes that section; session start sets each new worktree up
    git-pre-commit      retired: does nothing; an earlier pm's section in .pm/hooks/pre-commit runs it until pm upgrade removes that section

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm hook stop --help
usage: pm hook stop [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm hook owner-request --help
usage: pm hook owner-request [-h] [--runtime {claude,codex}]

Stop: block once while the reply asks the owner for something no open need or action of this session covers. A small model on the session's own runtime judges the reply: Claude Haiku through claude -p for Claude Code, gpt-5.6-luna through codex exec for Codex, each run from an empty temp directory with no hooks, no project instructions and no saved session. Each runtime's hook entry names its runtime; pm never guesses it. When the check cannot run (the runtime's CLI is missing, fails or times out), it exits 1 with the cause on stderr: Claude Code shows it, Codex lets the stop through.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --runtime {claude,codex}
                        the session's runtime, which the judge runs on; pm init writes claude in .claude/settings.json and codex in .codex/hooks.json (default: claude, as an entry written before this flag)
===== pm hook ask --help
usage: pm hook ask [-h] --runtime {claude,codex}

Raise the session's ask-tool question as a question need under the newest open task the session holds (else none), so the owner can answer it on the site as well as in the session; the first answer to close the need wins, and no transcript or rollout is read. Claude Code runs it on PermissionRequest of AskUserQuestion, beside the question's dialog, for up to a day: it raises the question and waits on one call to the pm service; on a site reply it closes the need as answered on the site and answers the tool with the reply, one "<question>: <answer>" line per question (an answer naming an option's label is that option; a reply with no such line answers every question whole); on a close elsewhere it exits; when Claude Code stops it (SIGTERM, on Esc or the session ending) it closes the need as dismissed. Claude Code runs it on PostToolUse of AskUserQuestion too: it closes the call's open question with the dialog's answers. A subagent's call steps aside. Codex runs it on PostToolUse of request_user_input_async, which returns at once: it raises the question once, by its call id (tool_use_id), and steps aside for any other tool; and on UserPromptSubmit: it closes the session's open questions with the prompt the owner typed, leaving out a message the pm service relayed. The pm service closes a Codex question as answered on the site before it queues the site's answer into the session. It fails open: on its own error it says why on stderr, prints nothing and exits 0.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --runtime {claude,codex}
                        the session's runtime; pm init writes claude in .claude/settings.json and codex in .codex/hooks.json
